comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-22T02:41:23.132Z","harness:claude-code","Duplicate check: pm search for 'read_output_cursor_stale context cursor flaky' returned the closed GH-1753 item (intent reads, fixed by binding cursors to first-pass rows) and the closed health replay isolation item pm-oahhyc; neither covers a plain context read going stale without a write."
  "2026-11-22T18:56:19.250Z","harness:claude-code","Investigation 2026-11-22 (evening): the wall-clock hypothesis is disproved; the cause is still unknown and this item stays open.\n\nMethod. A scratch tracker was seeded like the failing spec (30 in_progress tasks tagged matrix with long descriptions, one parented, 4 closed). The built CLI was run with a Node preload that shifts Date and Date.now by a fixed offset, so the replay sees a later clock than the first page.\n\nResult 1. context --limit 25 --tag matrix --output-budget unbounded returned byte-identical low_level and high_level collections at offsets of 1 s, 61 s, 1 h, 1 d, 7 d and 31 d (24 low-level rows each time). The now and window values moved with the offset, which confirms the preload was active. The only other difference in the whole payload was summary.in_progress_idle appearing at 31 d; summary is not part of a row collection and is not fingerprinted.\n\nResult 2. A first page issued with --output-budget 1500 gave a cursor for path low_level (offset 4 of 24). Replaying that cursor under offsets of 0 s, 30 s, 61 s, 1 h, 1 d and 31 d was accepted every time (exit 0, no read_output_cursor_stale).\n\nConclusion. No context row field is derived from the wall clock in a way that changes the low_level collection or its fingerprint, so a slow replay crossing a time bucket is not the cause. The second acceptance criterion (exclude a clock-derived field from the fingerprint) does not apply.\n\nWhat remains. The failing spec drives the CLI in process (runCliInProcess), so both the first page and the replay share module state inside one worker, and the failure appeared only under a loaded full coverage run. Candidates not yet tested: an in-process cache whose expiry depends on elapsed time and changes row order or row content between the two calls, and the intent second-pass binding (a cursor validates against the first pass's collection). The next step is to repeat the page and replay in process with a controlled delay and with coverage instrumentation, comparing the two bound collections row by row when the fingerprints differ.\n"
body: ""
